* text eol=lf

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Binary assets — exempt from the line-ending normalization above.
#
# `* text eol=lf` was applying to EVERY tracked file with no exclusions, including
# binaries. Git's text-normalization rewrites any \r\n byte pair to \n on checkout,
# and it does this blindly — it does not check whether the file is actually text.
#
# This corrupted frontend/src/app/favicon.ico: one of its embedded PNG frames opens
# with the signature bytes 89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A. The 0D 0A in there is
# indistinguishable to Git from a Windows line ending, so checkout silently rewrote
# it to 0A, deleting a byte and shifting everything after it in that frame. The
# corrupted signature, misread as a little-endian uint32, is 1196314761 — the exact
# number Turbopack reported in "Unknown bitmap header type (size=1196314761)" when
# it failed to build. The file was never intentionally edited; Git did this on a
# checkout, invisibly, with no error at the time.
#
# Every pattern below is listed explicitly rather than relying on Git's binary
# auto-detection, which is a heuristic and has already failed silently once here.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.webm binary

# The vendored pdf.js worker specifically, rather than a blanket `*.mjs binary`.
# eslint.config.mjs and postcss.config.mjs are genuine hand-edited text — marking
# every .mjs as binary would silently disable diffing them for no benefit, since
# they were never at risk (no \r\n byte sequences in normal JS source to mismatch).
# The vendored worker is a minified, machine-generated blob where that risk is real.
frontend/public/_vendor/** binary
